const PIANO_SOUND = `ROLE: piano-sound. Your port: 5196. Goal: playback that sounds like a real GRAND PIANO, with natural decay, damper release, dynamics and the sustain pedal.
You own: the playback side of src/features/viewer/** (synth.ts, player.ts, usePlayer.ts, clock.ts, originalTrack.ts and the playback rows of SettingsPanels.tsx), public/soundfonts/**, the sound constants of src/assets/images.ts (keep existing exports compiling), the sound entries of docs/CREDITS.md, the attribution in src/features/settings/credits.ts, and one "Pedal" toggle row in Ajustes > Reproducción (SettingsScreen.tsx, additive). abcjs stays for drawing the score.
1. Samples: a free, high-quality grand piano. Reference choice: Salamander Grand Piano V3 (Alexander Holm, Yamaha C5, CC BY 3.0); a lightweight subset (every third note, A0..C8) is published by the Tone.js project (github.com/Tonejs/audio, folder salamander). Verify license and author on the source pages, download, trim silence, keep 3-6 s tails, encode for a good size/quality balance, and store them under public/soundfonts/ (they are precached for offline use; keep the piano set within ~8 MB). Two or three velocity layers are welcome if available under the same license and size allows.
2. Renderer: replace abcjs's synth with your own OfflineAudioContext renderer that the existing player keeps using (one AudioBuffer, loops, A-B, seek, speed, cursor sync, 'Original' sound untouched). Per note: nearest sample shifted with playbackRate, gain and brightness from ScoreEvent.velocities (fallback ~0.7 until engine-score provides them), natural decay while held, a short damper release at note end (no hard cuts, no clicks), re-strikes of a sounding pitch damp the previous one. Sustain pedal: while Score.pedals covers a note's end (and Settings.sustainPedal is on), the note keeps ringing until the pedal lifts. Metronome and count-in clicks from the bundled percussion samples; volume from settings.playbackVolume. Report render time and memory for 30 s and 3 min at 1x and 0.5x.
3. Wire Settings.sustainPedal: a Toggle "Pedal" in the viewer's Reproducción tab and in Ajustes > Reproducción; also pass it to scoreToAbc for the pedal marks if engine-score exposes that option (check its report/exports while you work; it lands in parallel).
4. Drop the FluidR3 piano mp3s only if nothing uses them any more (keep the percussion), and update docs/CREDITS.md and the About credits (exact CC BY 3.0 attribution of the new samples).
5. Evidence for the client (they cannot hear it otherwise): render the three demos before (current abcjs/FluidR3) and after (new piano, with a synthetic pedal if Score.pedals is not there yet) to MP3: docs/screenshots/sound-<demo>-antes.mp3 and -ahora.mp3; plus a 15-20 s video WITH AUDIO of the viewer playing the Oda (screen frames + the rendered audio muxed with ffmpeg), light and dark: docs/screenshots/sound-visor.mp4 and sound-visor-dark.mp4. Objective checks since you cannot listen: per-note pitch within a few cents (FFT), no clipping, no clicks at note ends and loop points, smooth release envelopes, loudness comparable to before.`

const AUDIO_Q = `ROLE: audio-accuracy. Port: 5197 (check-transcription.mjs starts its own server; pass --url to reuse one). Goal: far fewer wrong notes on REAL material.
You own: src/engine/audio/**, src/engine/transcribe/**, test/fixtures/**, scripts/fixtures/**, public/models/**; you may add the credit line of new fixtures to docs/CREDITS.md.
1. Realistic benchmark (the current fixtures are too clean): (a) render public-domain pieces with REAL grand-piano samples (Salamander Grand Piano, CC BY 3.0: download your own copy to your temp folder; commit only the rendered fixtures, credited in their JSON "source" and in docs/CREDITS.md) with sustain pedal, dynamics, rubato (+-8 %) and room reverb, plus degraded versions like video audio: detune (+-20 to 45 cents), sped-up "TikTok" style (1.05-1.25x with pitch shift), phone-speaker band limit (about 300 Hz - 7 kHz) re-recorded by a microphone (noise floor, light compression). Keep it small (<= 12 files, <= 6 MB, 8-20 s each) with exact ground truth. (b) If you can find 1-3 real CC0/public-domain piano recordings with a public-domain score (e.g. Kimiko Ishizaka's Open Well-Tempered Clavier, CC0, BWV 846), use them for a qualitative check (score alignment by DTW is welcome, optional).
2. Measure the current pipeline: onset F1 (+-50 ms) per fixture and an error breakdown (missed, extra, octave errors, +-1 semitone errors, duration errors).
3. Improve, measuring each step and keeping only what helps: global tuning estimation and compensation (Basic Pitch contours have 3 bins per semitone; detect the offset, correct and re-decode or re-run) so detuned or sped-up audio lands on the right semitones (right sharps); thresholds and minimum durations for real piano with pedal (long overlapping notes, repeated notes under pedal); harmonic/octave-ghost suppression if it now helps on real samples; better note velocities (the new playback uses them).
4. Sandbox only, never add dependencies to the project: if a piano-specific in-browser model with a permissive license exists (e.g. Magenta.js Onsets and Frames, Apache-2.0), install it ONLY in your temp folder and compare it on the same benchmark: F1 by condition, model size, speed, memory. Report numbers and a recommendation; the lead and the client decide.
5. Keep all tests green, add tests for the new pieces, extend check-transcription.mjs with the new benchmark (means per condition), keep check-bundle.mjs passing.`

const SCORE_Q = `ROLE: score-accuracy. Port: 5198 (test/harness/abc.html for visual checks). Goal: scores that read right from real performances, with pedal marks and dynamics data for playback.
You own: src/engine/score/**, src/data/demos.ts, test/harness/abc*. Contract fields to implement (already declared in src/types.ts): Score.pedals, ScoreEvent.velocities, AnalysisResult.beatTimes.
1. Velocities first (the piano-sound agent needs them): ScoreEvent.velocities parallel to pitches, from the raw notes (merged notes keep the loudest). Tests.
2. Sustain pedal: infer damper-pedal spans from the raw NoteEvents (notes that keep sounding past later onsets in the same register beyond what fingers could hold; re-pedal at harmony changes), quantized to the beat grid -> Score.pedals (absolute ticks, sorted, non-overlapping). Notation: pedal marks under the bass staff in ABC (verify which abcjs decorations render Ped. / release, e.g. !ped! and !ped-up!, with abcjs.parseOnly and a render), behind an AbcOptions flag the viewer drives from Settings.sustainPedal; MusicXML <pedal type="start|stop"/>; MIDI CC64. Tests with synthetic pedalled streams (expected spans) and non-pedalled ones (no spans).
3. Rhythm on real performances: measure tempo, downbeat, meter and key on rubato material (build rubato streams yourself, and use the audio-accuracy agent's realistic fixtures when they appear in test/fixtures) and improve. A beat tracker that follows tempo changes (dynamic programming over onset strength) producing AnalysisResult.beatTimes that buildScore uses to map seconds to ticks is the expected direction; constant-tempo behaviour stays when beatTimes is absent. Expose a helper for the viewer's 'Original' sync (score tick -> source seconds through the beat map) and list the viewer change in your requests.
4. Keep every existing test green (ABC still validates with abcjs, buildScore < 30 ms for 3 000 notes), add tests for everything new, and screenshot before/after notation of a realistic fixture -> docs/screenshots/score-quality-*.png (light and dark).`

/* ---------- Phase D: reviewers that only report, then fixers per owner, then a final check ---------- */

const REVIEW_COMMON = `PHASE D REVIEWER. You only REPORT: never edit app files. Write only your screenshots (docs/screenshots/review-<area>-*.png) and your report. The app is feature-complete after phase C: read docs/agents/reports/*.json (integration.json first) and docs/CLIENT-FEEDBACK.md before you start.
Every finding has: severity (high | medium | low), location (file:line or screen + state), problem, why it matters, the concrete fix, and the owner who should apply it (BRIEF section 5). Rank by severity. No nitpicks the BRIEF or the client did not ask for.
Report: docs/agents/reports/review-<area>.json = { summary, findings: [{ severity, owner, location, problem, fix }], verification }, and return the same JSON.`

const REVIEW_DESIGN = `ROLE: review-design. Port 5191. Compare every screen and state (light and dark, 390x844 and 320x640) with docs/reference and BRIEF section 2: whitespace and one idea per screen; coherent radii, shadows, type scale and icon sizes; accent only for progress, active states and the one primary action; no emojis, no stray or explanatory text; Spanish copy; dark-mode legibility of the score; motion quality (durations, easing, reduced motion).`

const REVIEW_CODE = `ROLE: review-code. No server needed (port 5192 if you need one). Read src/** looking for real defects: AudioContext / worker / MediaStream leaks; abcjs synth and timer cleanup on unmount and on param changes; IndexedDB errors and transactions; async races (StrictMode double effects, setState after unmount, stale closures); error paths and their Spanish messages; offline precache of model, soundfont, images and fonts; bundle split (tfjs only in the worker chunk).`

const REVIEW_TRANSCRIPTION = `ROLE: review-transcription. Port 5193. Quality from audio to notation with test/fixtures: scripts/fixtures/check-transcription.mjs plus the real app flow (import each fixture). F1 per fixture; tempo, key and meter against the ground-truth JSON; readability of the resulting scores (difficulty modes, hand split, ties, accidentals), with screenshots.`

const REVIEW_MOBILE = `ROLE: review-mobile. Port 5194. Mobile usability: 44 px touch targets; safe areas (emulate a notch); sheets and dialogs (focus, scroll, drag to dismiss); empty, loading and error states on every screen; labels, roles and focus order; 320 px width; offline after the first load (production preview + service worker).`

const FIX = (owner) => `PHASE D FIXER (owner: ${owner}). Apply the findings assigned to "${owner}" in docs/agents/reports/review-*.json, highest severity first, only inside your ownership (BRIEF section 5). Verify each fix (typecheck, tests, screenshots light and dark) and report which findings you fixed, which you rejected and why. Report file: docs/agents/reports/fix-${owner}.json.`

const FINAL = `PHASE D FINAL CHECK. Port 5189. npm run typecheck, npm test, npm run build and node scripts/e2e.mjs must all pass; refresh docs/screenshots/final-*.png (light and dark) and README.md. Fix only small regressions (you may edit any file); report anything bigger. Report file: docs/agents/reports/final.json.`

/* ---------- Runner: the order of work (the Windows lead runs it by hand with the Agent tool) ---------- */

const REVIEW = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    summary: { type: 'string' },
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: { severity: { type: 'string' }, owner: { type: 'string' }, location: { type: 'string' }, problem: { type: 'string' }, fix: { type: 'string' } },
        required: ['severity', 'owner', 'location', 'problem', 'fix'],
      },
    },
    verification: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['summary', 'findings', 'verification'],
}

const run = (role, phaseName, prompt, schema = REPORT) =>
  agent(COMMON + '\n\n' + prompt, { label: role, phase: phaseName, schema })
    .then((r) => (r ? { role, ...r } : { role, failed: true }))

const upstream = (...roles) =>
  'UPSTREAM REPORTS (agents whose work you depend on): read ' + roles.map((r) => `docs/agents/reports/${r}.json`).join(', ') + ' first.'

// Foundations ran on Linux: ui and assets finished (reports in docs/agents/reports/); the engines stopped halfway.
phase('A · Engines')
const scoreP = run('engine-score', 'A · Engines', SCORE + '\n\n' + SCORE_A)
const audioP = run('engine-audio', 'A · Engines', AUDIO + '\n\n' + AUDIO_A)

phase('B · Screens')
const settingsP = run('settings', 'B · Screens', SETTINGS + '\n\n' + SETTINGS_B + '\n\n' + upstream('ui', 'assets'))
const libraryP = scoreP.then(() => run('library', 'B · Screens', LIBRARY + '\n\n' + LIBRARY_B + '\n\n' + upstream('ui', 'assets', 'engine-score')))
const viewerP = scoreP.then(() => run('viewer', 'B · Screens', VIEWER + '\n\n' + VIEWER_B + '\n\n' + upstream('ui', 'assets', 'engine-score')))
const captureP = Promise.all([scoreP, audioP]).then(() =>
  run('capture', 'B · Screens', CAPTURE + '\n\n' + CAPTURE_B + '\n\n' + upstream('ui', 'assets', 'engine-audio', 'engine-score')),
)

const reports = await Promise.all([scoreP, audioP, settingsP, libraryP, viewerP, captureP])
const failed = reports.filter((r) => r.failed).map((r) => r.role)
log('Build agents done. Failed: ' + (failed.length ? failed.join(', ') : 'none'))

phase('C · Integrate')
const all = ['ui', 'assets', 'engine-audio', 'engine-score', 'settings', 'library', 'viewer', 'capture']
const integration = await run('integration', 'C · Integrate', INTEGRATION + '\n\n' + upstream(...all))

phase('D · Review')
// The client's "Compartir con Pauta" (SHARE) is built in parallel with the reviewers; the fixers start after it.
const shareP = run('share', 'D · Review', SHARE + '\n\n' + upstream('ui', 'engine-audio', 'capture', 'integration'))
const reviews = await Promise.all(
  [
    ['review-design', REVIEW_DESIGN],
    ['review-code', REVIEW_CODE],
    ['review-transcription', REVIEW_TRANSCRIPTION],
    ['review-mobile', REVIEW_MOBILE],
  ].map(([role, prompt]) => run(role, 'D · Review', REVIEW_COMMON + '\n\n' + prompt, REVIEW)),
)
// One fixer per owner that received findings, in parallel (owners never share files).
const owners = [...new Set(reviews.flatMap((r) => (r.findings ?? []).map((f) => f.owner)))]
const share = await shareP
const fixes = await Promise.all(owners.map((owner) => run('fix-' + owner, 'D · Review', FIX(owner))))
const final = await run('final', 'D · Review', FINAL)
return { reports, integration, share, reviews, fixes, final }
